[Event "KID: Classical"]

{The King's Indian lets White build a big centre, then strikes with ...e5 (or ...c5) and storms the kingside with ...f5–f4–g5. Sharp, double-edged, a favourite of Fischer and Kasparov.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... g6 {[%csl Gg7] The King's Indian fianchetto. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
3... Bg7 {[%cal Gg7d4] The bishop eyes d4 and the long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4. e4 {The big centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
4... d6 {Controls e5, prepares ...e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
5. Nf3 {The Classical. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Be2 {Develops the bishop to e2, where it controls d3 and supports the knight on f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] The thematic strike. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
7. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
7... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Pressure on d4, forcing a decision. Develops the knight to c6, where it supports the pawn on e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
8. d5 {Closes the centre; White will attack on the queenside.}
8... Ne7 {Heads for the kingside. Moves the knight out of attack to e7 and eyes the pawn on d5. White's last move put a pawn on d5, which this now pressures.}
9. Ne1 {Re-routes to d3 to support c5 and f4. Regroups the knight to e1.}
 (9. b4 {The Bayonet Attack. Gains space on the queenside; the pawn on b4 takes a5 and c5 away from the opponent.}
9... Nh5 {Prepares ...f5 and eyes f4. The knight goes to h5.}
10. Re1 {Prepares Bf1 and keeps e4 solid. The rook goes to e1.}
)
9... Nd7 {Clears the f-pawn. Regroups the knight to d7 and supports the pawn on e5.}
10. Nd3 {Supports c5. Develops the knight to d3, where it eyes the pawn on e5.}
10... f5 {[%cal Gf5e4] The kingside storm begins. Supports the centre: the pawn on f5 controls g4 and eyes the pawn on e4.} *

[Event "KID: Sämisch, Four Pawns & Fianchetto"]

{The other main White systems against the King's Indian.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. c4 {Space. Supports the centre: the pawn on c4 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
2... g6 {KID. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
3. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
 (3. Nf3 {Fianchetto system coming. Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
3... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4. g3 {Fianchetto. The pawn on g3 controls f4 and h4.}
4... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
5. Bg2 {Long diagonal. Fianchettoes the bishop on g2, where it rakes the long diagonal through f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
5... d6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
6. O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6... Nbd7 {Prepares ...e5. Develops the knight to d7, where it controls e5.}
7. Nc3 {Develops the knight to c3, where it controls d5 and e4.}
7... e5 {Strikes the centre. The pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
8. e4 {Classical centre. The pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5.}
)
3... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4. e4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on e4 controls d5 and f5. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
4... d6 {Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
5. f3 {The Sämisch. Supports the centre: the pawn on f3 controls g4 and supports the pawn on e4.}
 (5. f4 {The Four Pawns. Supports the centre: the pawn on f4 controls e5 and g5.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
6... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the overextended centre. The pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
7. d5 {Gains space. Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c6 and e6.}
7... e6 {Opens lines. Supports the centre: the pawn on e6 controls f5 and eyes the pawn on d5. White's last move put a pawn on d5, which this now pressures.}
8. Be2 {Develops the bishop to e2, where it controls d3 and supports the knight on f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
8... exd5 {Takes the pawn on d5; White will want to recapture.}
9. cxd5 {Recaptures on d5, restoring the material balance.}
)
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Be3 {Develops the bishop to e3, where it supports the pawn on d4.}
6... e5 {Strikes. Claims the centre: the pawn on e5 controls f4 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
7. Nge2 {Develops the knight to e2, where it supports the pawn on d4.}
7... c6 {Prepares ...b5 or ...cxd5. Supports the centre: the pawn on c6 controls b5 and d5. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
8. Qd2 {Prepares O-O-O. The queen goes to d2 and supports the pawn on d4.}
8... Nbd7 {Develops the knight to d7, where it supports the pawn on e5.} *

[Event "KID vs London"]

{Against the London, Black plays a King's Indian setup with ...c5.}
1. d4 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... Nf6 {Develops the knight to f6, where it controls e4 and d5.}
2. Nf3 {Develops the knight to f3, where it controls e5 and supports the pawn on d4.}
2... g6 {KID setup. The pawn on g6 controls f5 and h5.}
3. Bf4 {The London. Develops the bishop to f4, where it controls e5.}
3... Bg7 {Fianchettoes the bishop on g7, where it rakes the long diagonal through f6. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
4. e3 {Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the bishop on f1.}
4... d6 {Prepares ...Nbd7 and ...e5. Supports the centre: the pawn on d6 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8.}
5. h3 {Keeps the bishop safe from ...Nh5. The pawn on h3 controls g4.}
5... O-O {Castles. The king is safe and the rook comes into play.}
6. Be2 {Develops the bishop to e2, where it controls d3 and supports the knight on f3. That clears the way to castle kingside.}
6... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the centre. The pawn on c5 controls b4 and eyes the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d8.}
7. c3 {Supports d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.} *
